            "<p>Heat Energy</p>",
            "<p>Energy produced by heat</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Electrical Energy</p>",
            "<p>Energy from electric current</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Chemical Energy</p>",
            "<p>Energy stored in fuels and food</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Light Energy</p>",
            "<p>Energy from sunlight</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "oo2htvm",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. Kinetic Energy"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "uq01fxn",
      "content": "Kinetic energy is the energy possessed by an object due to its motion."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "ht813pn",
      "content": "Formula: KE = ½ mv²"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "dj5bmqo",
      "content": "Where:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "2izxmk5",
      "content": "m = mass of the object"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "pxoryf6",
      "content": "v = velocity of the object"
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "Energy can neither be created nor destroyed; it can only be transformed from one form to another.",
      "id": "637qs5f"
    },
    {
      "id": "gxqy916",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. Potential Energy"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "q5ab006",
      "content": "Potential energy is the energy possessed by an object due to its position or configuration."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "r3xhqv1",
      "content": "Formula: PE = mgh"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "avvugwq",
      "content": "Where:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "bmj7k1g",
      "content": "m = mass"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "b2hoy74",
      "content": "g = acceleration due to gravity"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "41nspty",
      "content": "h = height"
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Property",
        "Kinetic Energy",
        "Potential Energy"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Definition",
          "Energy possessed due to motion",
          "Energy possessed due to position or state"
        ],
        [
          "Formula",
          "KE = 1/2 m v²",
          "PE = m g h"
        ],
        [
          "Key Variable",
          "Velocity (v)",
          "Height (h) / Position"
        ],
        [
          "Example",
          "A rolling stone or flying arrow",
          "Water stored behind a dam"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "ezr5gpn"
    },
    {
      "id": "i85ea0y",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5. Law of Conservation of Energy"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "7qjh31a",
      "content": "The law of conservation of energy states that energy can neither be created nor destroyed; it can only be converted from one form to another."
    },
    {
      "id": "qehgk7i",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "6. Mechanical Energy"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6m31jji",
      "content": "Mechanical energy is the sum of kinetic energy and potential energy."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "mkrkvtv",
      "content": "Mechanical Energy = KE + PE"
    },
    {
      "id": "b2zzkvy",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "7. Meaning of Power"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6z4e1or",
      "content": "Power is the rate at which work is done or energy is transferred."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ftzodua",
      "content": "Formula: Power = Work / Time"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "y83vqvb",
      "content": "SI Unit of Power = Watt (W)"
    },
    {
      "id": "3cbgse8",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "8. Units of Power"
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Remember that 1 Horsepower (hp) = 746 Watts; this conversion is frequently asked in numerical problems.",
        "1 Kilowatt-hour (kWh) is the commercial unit of electric energy and equals 3.6 × 10⁶ Joules.",
        "Mechanical Energy is the sum total of Potential Energy and Kinetic Energy."
      ],
      "id": "gsvptbk"
    },
